WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.260
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents. Episode 79 is June 13th, 2026. Another day,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:12.000
another round of AI developments. Let's break it down. US export controls just forced and

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:18.600
thropic to pull its newest frontier models offline for every user worldwide. The US government

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.640
issued an export control directive requiring anthropic to suspend all access to Fable 5 and Mythos 5

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:31.020
by any foreign national, including its own employees. The company responded by disabling the two

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.500
newest models for every customer to maintain compliance, while all other clawed models remain

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.560
available. The directive cites national security authorities and applies both inside and outside

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.800
the United States. Anthropic called the order a misunderstanding and stated it is working to restore

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.360
access. Builders relying on the latest clawed releases for agentic coding or research tasks must

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:02.220
immediately re-root workloads to unaffected models or alternative providers. This continues the safety

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.960
and policy developments from recent episodes, where regulatory actions are directly altering which

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:16.540
frontier models stay accessible. Moonshot AI open-sourced Kimi K2.7 code, a coding model that reduces

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:22.260
thinking token usage by roughly 30% while claiming double-digit gains on internal benchmarks.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:30.240
That brings us to the model updates. Moonshot AI released Kimi K2.7 code under a modified

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:37.420
MIT license as an open source update to its K2.6 coding model. The new model uses the same

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:44.180
trillion parameter M-extra of experts architecture, adds a 256,000 token context window,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:52.700
and reduces reasoning token usage by approximately 30%. It reports gains of 21.8% on Kimi code

00:01:52.700 --> 00:02:01.080
bench V2, 11% on program bench, and 31.5% on MLS bench light, all proprietary benchmarks.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.480
The model runs exclusively in thinking mode with temperature fixed at 1.0 and is available

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.120
through the Kimi API and Kimi code. This moves the open-weight models conversation forward by showing

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:20.820
concrete token efficiency gains on coding benchmarks. Open AI began rolling out the ability for go,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:27.820
plus pro, and business users to bank codex rate limit resets for later use, starting each with

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.940
one free reset. Plus, and pro users can also invite up to three friends to try codex for two weeks.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.800
Both parties receive an extra banked reset when the friend sends their first message.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.160
The changes address user requests for more flexible usage timing. Developers running

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.720
sustained codex workloads can now plan around saved resets rather than daily explorations.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:56.420
Now for agent and tool developments, GitHub updated co-pilot CLI with better orchestration logic

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:02.800
that reduces unnecessary hand-offs between agents. The change improves progress speed on multi-step

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.620
tasks without requiring users to adjust any new settings. It builds on existing delegation patterns

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:15.120
while tightening when the CLI decides to pass work to another component. Teams using co-pilot CLI

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.700
for terminal-based coding can test the update immediately for fewer context switches.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.840
This fits the ongoing agents and tool use arc, where small refinements to orchestration

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:32.860
are improving reliability on multi-step workflows. NanoClaw agents now root all package, CLI

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:39.020
tool, and MCP server requests exclusively through jfrog registries, blocking installation of

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:45.800
unvetted or malicious dependencies. When a request is rejected, the agent receives a 403 policy error

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:52.120
and is guided to an approved alternative. The integration is free for open source users via jfrog's

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:58.000
public-vetted registry and plugs directly into enterprise jfrog environments for compliance tracking.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:04.520
Operators gain a system of record for every package and agent consumes without manual approval steps.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.540
UC Berkeley, Princeton, EP, FL, and Databricks researchers released PixelRag,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.460
which renders web pages as screenshots, indexes image tiles with Quinn 3VL embedding 2B

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:26.180
and feeds them to vision language models. On simple QA, it reaches 78.8% accuracy versus 71.6%

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.060
for the strongest text parser, with similar gains on table and multi-modal tasks.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:39.400
An agent using PixelRag consumed 3.6 million prompt tokens versus 37.5 million for text retrieval

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:46.020
on the same workload. The system requires Quinn 3VL 4B class models or larger.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:51.620
Smaller models lose the accuracy advantage. In the practical and community section,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:59.380
a community developer has made available a fresh C++ port of paddle OCR versions 3-6 that

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:05.420
leverages the NCNN inference engine. This implementation eliminates reliance on the resource

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:12.000
intensive official paddle C++ runtime components. As a result, setup for OCR functionality

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.260
becomes more straightforward and execution times improve. The full source code along with usage

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:24.700
examples resides in a GitHub repository maintained by the original contributor. Anyone working on

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.840
device local or minimal dependency OCR applications can integrate this solution directly.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.780
It bypasses the need to handle the complete paddle software environment during deployment.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.940
The release was shared via a post in the machine learning subreddit.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.720
Allen AI has introduced Olmo EVAL as a dedicated evaluation workbench.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:50.460
The tool is structured to integrate directly into cycles of model development and refinement.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.860
It provides standardized metrics along with logging capabilities for consistent tracking.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.760
Open models benefit from this setup during rapid experimentation phases.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.540
Teams engaged in training or fine-tuning processes can utilize it to maintain uniform

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.700
evaluation standards. No requirement exists to construct bespoke evaluation harnesses when

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:18.980
using this workbench. Details on initial configuration appear in the Allen AI portion of the

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:24.060
hugging face blog. Okay, let's pop the hood on this faithful uncertainty technique from the

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.640
Google researchers. Everyone talks about reducing hallucinations as if the only options are

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.140
answer everything or refuse when uncertain. In practice, faithful uncertainty separates a model's

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:41.200
internal statistical confidence from the linguistic signals it uses to express doubt.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.960
The core mechanism trains the model to output hedging phrases such as

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:51.760
my best guess is only when it's own token probabilities for the factual claim fall below a

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.800
learned threshold. This alignment avoids the utility tax that occurs when strict

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.800
abstention discards large numbers of correct answers the model actually knows. Adding the technique

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.620
requires supervised fine-tuning on uncertainty labeled data which creates a bootstrapping problem

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.920
because the correct label depends on what that specific model knows at training time.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.380
In agentic systems, the payoff appears in tool use decisions. The model can decide whether to

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:25.400
invoke search or trusted parameters without external classifiers. The practical limit is that

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:31.420
prompting alone cannot fully close the gap. Reinforcement learning on uncertainty aware trajectories

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:37.360
is still required for production reliability. Teams should adopt faithful uncertainty prompting

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:44.040
today for low stakes chat while planning RL stages for any long horizon agent. This reasoning

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:49.460
models development points toward future stages that bake metacognition deeper into frontier models.

